# 26 — A tilted orbit in 3-D, written as one vector equation
#
# Newton's law of gravity for a planet at position r (a 3-vector) around the Sun:
#
#     r'' = -G M☉ r / |r|³
#
# One line covers all three coordinates: `solve` sees that r(0) is a 3-vector
# and follows x, y and z together. We launch the planet with part of its speed
# out of the x-y plane, so the orbit is a tilted ellipse.
#
# Two things must not change along the way (per kilogram of planet):
#   * the energy             E = ½ |v|² − G M☉ / |r|
#   * the angular momentum   L = r × v   (a vector: its direction is the orbit's axis)

GM = G M_sun
r0 = <1, 0, 0> AU
v0 = <0, 25, 12> km/s        # 27.7 km/s: slower than a circular orbit (29.8 km/s),
                             # so 1 AU is the farthest point and the planet falls inward

solve r'' = -GM r / |r|³
  with r(0) = r0, r'(0) = v0
  for t from 0 s to 3 yr

E(t) = ½ |r'(t)|² - GM / |r(t)|
L(t) = r(t) × r'(t)

T = 3 yr
print "energy at the start:", E(0 s) in MJ/kg to 6 digits
print "relative energy drift after 3 years:", |E(T) - E(0 s)| / |E(0 s)|
print "angular momentum at the start:", L(0 s) to 6 digits
print "relative change in L after 3 years:", |L(T) - L(0 s)| / |L(0 s)|

# The orbit is a flat ellipse in a tilted plane: its axis L makes this angle with the z-axis
print "tilt of the orbit:", acos(L(0 s).z / |L(0 s)|) in deg to 6 digits

# Kepler's third law from the energy: a = -GM / (2E), T² = 4π² a³ / GM
a = -GM / (2 E(0 s))
print "semi-major axis:", a in AU to 6 digits
print "period:", 2π √(a³ / GM) in yr to 6 digits

assert |E(T) - E(0 s)| < 1e-6 |E(0 s)|, "energy should be conserved"
assert |L(T) - L(0 s)| < 1e-6 |L(0 s)|, "angular momentum should be conserved"
assert |r(T) · L(0 s)| < 1e-6 |r(T)| |L(0 s)|, "the orbit should stay in its plane (r ⊥ L)"

plot r.y in AU vs r.x in AU to "gallery/orbit_3d.png"
